.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-MOK-REPAIR 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-mok-repair
.Nd see which Secure Boot machine owner keys are enrolled, and put the install's own key back when it is not
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm status
.Nm
.Cm repair
.Op Fl -force
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
With Secure Boot on, a kldload machine boots only if its own machine owner
key is enrolled: that key signs ZFSBootMenu and the ZFS and NVIDIA
modules.
Enrolment needs someone at the console, in MokManager's ten-second
window, with the right password, so it is easy to miss; and every
reinstall leaves its old key behind, so keys pile up while the one that
matters may never be enrolled.
The result is
.Dq Secure Boot validation failed
with no system to fix it from.
.Pp
It runs on the installed machine, or from the live image: there it
imports the installed pool read-only and uses that key.
It needs root.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm status
The default.
The firmware's Secure Boot state; how many keys are enrolled and how many
are kldload install keys; pending requests; the install's key and whether
it is enrolled, queued, or neither; and any loaded module signed by a
different enrolled key.
.It Cm repair
Queue a reset of the whole key list, then an enrolment of the install's
key \(em in that order, since a reset also discards anything queued \(em
and print what to do at the next boot:
.Dq Reset MOK list ,
then
.Dq Enroll MOK ,
each with the password
.Ql kldload .
On a terminal it offers to reboot.
.Pp
It refuses while a loaded module is signed by an enrolled key other than
the install's: the reset would stop that module loading.
Rebuild such modules with DKMS, which signs with the install key, or give
.Fl -force .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/lib/dkms/mok.der
The install's key.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Reported, or the requests are queued.
.It 1
No EFI, no
.Xr mokutil 1 ,
no install key, or
.Cm repair
refused.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
On a kldload desktop on 2026-09-30:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-mok-repair
── Secure Boot ─────────────────────────────
SecureBoot enabled
Enrolled MOKs: 4 total, 2 kldload install key(s)
⚠ 2 kldload keys enrolled — only the newest install's key is in use; the rest are stale NVRAM bloat.
Pending enrollment requests: 0
Install key: /var/lib/dkms/mok.der
  subject=CN=kldload-mok-20260830133808-430b62b703c15c02
✓ this key is already enrolled — Secure Boot should verify the boot chain.
Loaded modules signed by another enrolled key: none
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
The same machine, with
.Xr modinfo 8
replaced by a script that reports the NVIDIA module as signed by the
.Dq DKMS module signing key
the machine also has enrolled:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-mok-repair repair
Not repairing: these loaded modules are signed by another enrolled key,
and the reset would stop them loading with Secure Boot on:
    nvidia (DKMS module signing key)
Rebuild them with DKMS (which signs with the install key) first, or run
  kldload-mok-repair repair --force
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Pp
Nothing was queued.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mokutil 1 ,
.Xr kldload-ca 8 ,
.Xr kldload-secure-boot 8
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-08-02 a lab machine had seventeen stale keys enrolled.
On 2026-08-03 its key check read
.Xr mokutil 1 Ns 's
exit status, which is inverted, and its count took issuer lines for
subjects.
On 2026-08-10 a repair queued over ssh reported failure because it asked
a question with no one to answer.
.Pp
On 2026-09-30, writing this page:
.Cm repair
reset every key without looking at what the loaded modules were signed
with.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
A reset removes every enrolled key, the distribution's certificate among
them; shim carries its own vendor certificate, so the distribution's
kernel still boots.
.Pp
The password is fixed at
.Ql kldload ,
whatever
.Ev KLDLOAD_MOK_PASSWORD
was at install.
